{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah, Issachar, Zebulun, and Benjamin.</b> He mentioned the first six, who are the sons of Leah, in the order of their generations. And it would have been fitting to mention Benjamin last of all his brothers, for he is the youngest of them all, and the reason he mentioned him seventh is because the lower world is divided into seven climes, and the seventh clime is the Land of Israel, which is the center of the inhabited world, and it is the point tempered with cold and heat more than all the other lands because it is central to the extremes. Therefore Benjamin was written seventh, to allude to the Temple that is in the seventh clime, which is in the portion of Benjamin. And even though the philosophical scholars who arranged the climes wrote in some of their treatises that the Land of Israel is in the fourth clime, it all amounts to the same thing: for since that land is the point and there are three climes on one side and three climes on the other side, it is fourth to each set of three and three, and it is central, and it is seventh. And this resembles the day of Shabbat, which is central to the days of the week and is the seventh day. And our Rabbis of blessed memory did not need to inform us that the luminaries were suspended on the fourth day except to proclaim the excellence of Shabbat." "<b>And know</b> that the eve of Shabbat begins from the fourth day, and when you count Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday before Shabbat, and Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday after Shabbat — Shabbat, which is seventh, is also central and also fourth to each set of three. And likewise the Temple that is in this clime is central and seventh. And he mentioned Joseph last for two reasons: the first, so that the sons of the mistress would not lord it over the sons of the maidservants; therefore Dan and Naphtali, Gad and Asher, who are the sons of the maidservants, were written in the middle between Benjamin and Joseph. And the second, because Joseph was the greatest of them all in rank and honor, therefore he was mentioned last, in the way of lowliness; for such is the way of the righteous: all the while that the Holy One, blessed be He, adds to them honor and greatness, they add for themselves lowliness and humility. Thus we find with Joshua son of Nun: after Moses our master, peace be upon him, laid his hands upon him and he was appointed leader over Israel during Moses' lifetime, what is written there? (Deuteronomy 32:44) ״וידבר משה את כל דברי השירה הזאת באזני העם הוא והושע בן נון״ — 'And Moses spoke all the words of this song in the ears of the people, he and Hosea son of Nun.' Scripture mentions Hosea to teach you that he did not grow proud because of his distinction, but humbled himself." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah — all the tribes, all of them, were called by names of redemption: Reuben — (Exodus 3:7) ״ראה ראיתי את עני עמי״ — 'I have surely seen the affliction of My people'; Simeon — (Exodus 2:24) ״וישמע אלהים את נאקתם״ — 'and Elohim heard their groaning'; Levi — (Zechariah 2:15) ״ונלוו גוים רבים אל ה׳״ — 'and many nations shall join themselves to HaShem'; Judah — (Isaiah 12:1) ״אודך ה׳ כי אנפת בי״ — 'I will give thanks to You, HaShem, for though You were angry with me'; Issachar — (Jeremiah 31:16) ״כי יש שכר לפעולתך״ — 'for there is a reward for your work'; Zebulun — (I Kings 8:13) ״בנה בניתי בית זבול לך״ — 'I have surely built You a house of habitation'; Benjamin — (Isaiah 62:8) ״נשבע ה׳ בימינו״ — 'HaShem has sworn by His right hand'; Dan — (Genesis 15:14) ״וגם את הגוי אשר יעבדו דן אנכי״ — 'and also the nation whom they shall serve, I will judge'; Naphtali — by the name (Song of Songs 4:11) ״נפת תטופנה שפתותיך״ — 'honey and milk are under your tongue'; Gad — by the name (Numbers 11:7) ״והמן כזרע גד הוא״ — 'and the manna was like coriander seed'; Asher — by the name (Malachi 3:12) ״ואשרו אתכם כל הגוים כי תהיו אתם ארץ חפץ״ — 'and all the nations shall call you happy, for you shall be a desirable land'; and Joseph — by the name (Isaiah 11:11) ״יוסיף ה׳ שנית ידו״ — 'HaShem shall set His hand again a second time.' Thus they expounded in Tanhuma. And these twelve tribes — because they were destined to multiply to sixty myriads in the exodus from Egypt, you will find that the initial letters of their names add up to sixty myriads less three thousand, and Levi is not included among them, for he is not included in the tally of the children of Israel," "<b>And thus</b> they expounded in Pesikta: If you seek to know what the number of Israel is, take the initial letters of the tribes and you will know their number: the ר of Reuben — two hundred thousand; the ש of Simeon — three hundred thousand; the נ of Naphtali — fifty thousand; and so all the rest — it turns out that they add up to sixty myriads less three thousand. And those three thousand that are lacking are those who fell at the calf. And in Tanhuma they expounded thus in the parashah of Ki Tissa."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:21.900771646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}